  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 251 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nLight showers sprinkled across the area and abundant cloud cover \nhave allowed temperatures over the Denver metro and plains to linger \nin just the low 80s. The mountains have seen a bit more sunshine as \na shortwave continues push east However, daytime heating and \nmonsoonal moisture have quickly promoted the development of showers \nand storms over the peaks and ridges with winds occasionally gusting \nto 30-40 mph. Though an isolated shower cannot be ruled out for the \nDenver metro late this afternoon, the best chance of rain at low \nelevations will be over the easternmost Colorado plains, where \nbetter instability and moisture are in place. \n\nA cold front will move in from the north late tonight, bringing a \nperiod of breezy northerly winds in the early morning hours. High-\nres model guidance has trended drier with this cold front, limiting \nthe development of a persistent stratus layer. However, patchy low \nclouds will likely still develop east of Denver and erode just after \nsunrise. We expect temperatures on Tuesday to stay below normal, \nwith highs in the mid 70s at low elevations. Even with precipitable \nwater (PW) values in the 90th percentile over much of the state, the \npost-frontal airmass over the plains will struggle to destabilize \nleaving the best chance for showers in the mountains.\n\nA closed upper-level low pressure will begin to slide through the \nPacific Northwest and Great Basin region on Wednesday, turning flow \naloft south-southwesterly. Modest monsoonal moisture transport will \nbring PW values as high as 0.80-1.00\" to the mountains with higher \nvalues possible for the eastern Colorado plains, where southerly \nsurface winds may advect additional moisture. Therefore, Thursday \nstill looks to be the best chance for meaningful rainfall amounts\nover the Denver metro. The flash flooding threat appears to be \nlocalized in nature given the very shallow warm-cloud layer and \nmarginal storm motions (LCL-EL mean winds ~18KT).\n\nThe evolution of a few shortwave troughs over the Pacific Northwest \nFriday through Sunday remains unclear. However, there will likely be \nenough moisture for daily precipitation chances, particularly over \nthe high terrain. Temperatures will remain near-normal or even \ncooler than normal, with highs in the upper 70s and low 80s through \nthe weekend.",
